WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.269 --> 00:00:06.399
Thrills, spills, chills, and kills: this is
why we go to the movies, to see big, amazing,

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:09.400
improbable and/or impossible stuff happen
on the big screen.

00:00:09.670 --> 00:00:13.680
It gets the blood pumping to see impressive
feats of stunt work and death defiance—which

00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:16.150
is why tons of action movies come out every
year.

00:00:16.150 --> 00:00:20.290
While many are certainly satisfying, only
a few contain that elusive combination of

00:00:20.290 --> 00:00:24.640
a great story, unforgettable characters, and
the unmatched technical mastery necessary

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:27.580
to transcend genre thrills and achieve great
cinema.

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:30.760
Here are Looper's picks for the best action
movies ever made.

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:32.420
Die Hard

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:37.600
Part of the reason 1988's Die Hard works so
well is its cinematic context.

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:42.420
Action movies at the time all tended to feature
stoic dudes with huge muscles laying waste

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:47.440
with boulder-sized fists and machine guns,
never doubting their utter alpha maleness

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:49.140
and barely cracking a smile.

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:53.700
Contrast that with Die Hard, in which Bruce
Willis is a relatively normal-sized, normal

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:58.469
looking guy who cracks wise and expresses
fear and self-doubt as he almost single-handedly

00:00:58.469 --> 00:01:01.420
beats back terrorists to literally save Christmas.

00:01:01.690 --> 00:01:06.860
Die Hard also gave us a breakout performance
from the beloved Alan Rickman as Hans Gruber,

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:08.630
one of the all-time great movie villains …

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:11.480
"Efficient, adult, cooperative — not a lot
to ask.

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:16.780
Alas, your Mr. Takagi did not see it that
way, so he won't be joining us for the rest

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:17.420
of his life."

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:19.320
The Dark Knight

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:23.780
Christopher Nolan's "Dark Knight" trilogy
is really more like one long movie, but the

00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:27.959
middle part is definitely the best chapter,
showing the result of Bruce Wayne's training

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:31.980
in Batman Begins, and the start of what will
play out in The Dark Knight Rises.

00:01:32.369 --> 00:01:37.959
2008's The Dark Knight is arguably the best-made
superhero movie of all time, with a tone that

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:41.880
reflects the character and shows utter faithfulness
to the comics it's based on …

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:43.599
"What would I do without you?

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:45.320
Go back to ripping off mob dealers?

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:46.900
No, no, no, no.

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:51.340
no, you...You — you complete me."

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:53.120
The French Connection

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:58.079
Gene Hackman's tough guy cop Jimmy "Popeye"
Doyle just never stops running.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:01:58.800
Or driving.

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:03.219
Or roughing up criminals in the pursuit of
justice, even if he has to don a Santa suit

00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:04.219
while doing so …

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:07.300
"All winter long I gotta listen to him gripe
about his bowling scores.

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:11.120
Now I'm gonna bust your ass for those three
bags and I'm going to nail you for picking

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:11.980
your feet in Poughkeepsie!"

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:18.670
Despite being so frenetic, so tough, so new,
and so very violent, 1971's The French Connection

00:02:18.670 --> 00:02:23.460
won Best Picture at the Academy Awards — the
first R-rated flick ever to do so.

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:24.760
The Bourne Identity

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:31.800
In 2002, as the James Bond franchise was slumping
its way through an era of stale, lazily delivered

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:36.540
clichés, The Bourne Identity hit theaters
— a refreshingly modern, wholly American

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:40.480
spy movie that reflected a more modern environment
of geopolitics.

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:45.360
Matt Damon's ultra-trained super warrior doesn't
know who he is…but he's definitely aware

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:47.020
of his own incredible fighting abilities.

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:52.220
Thanks to the paranoid, shaky camerawork and
urgent pace, the audience rarely knows more

00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:56.580
than Jason Bourne does, and as a result they
never quite get to take a breath, either.

00:02:56.980 --> 00:02:58.160
Raiders of the Lost Ark

00:02:58.510 --> 00:03:03.040
It's supposed to be an homage to the action-adventure
serials that director Steven Spielberg and

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:05.780
producer George Lucas grew up watching in
the 1950s.

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:11.300
But the thing is, those often weren't very
good movies—1981's Raiders of the Lost Ark,

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:15.910
however, completely overshadows its source
material and is nearly a perfect film.

00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:19.220
Every scene is crowd-pleasing, particularly
the iconic action sequences …

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:28.760
Raiders is pure fun, beginning to end.

00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:30.360
RoboCop

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:36.660
1987's RoboCop is a violent, action-packed,
futuristic cop movie…or is it a violent,

00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:41.880
action-packed, futuristic cop movie that satirizes
violent, action-packed movies from the '80s?

00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:44.240
"You have the right to remain silent."

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:47.020
"F--- you!"

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:51.740
Like any good work of satire, it works on
both levels.

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:56.110
RoboCop has a lot to say about the value of
human life in the crime-ridden future world

00:03:56.110 --> 00:03:56.980
of New Detroit.

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:01.860
After all, it's about a cop struck down by
some pretty intense violence…and then resurrected

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:05.580
as a cyborg designed to execute as many criminals
as humanly possible.

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:07.360
The Matrix

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:10.860
A college-level philosophy class was never
so eye-popping.

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:15.760
1999's The Matrix kind of blew everybody's
minds with its central conceit: that there's

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:19.340
no point to human life beyond their bodies
being bags of energy.

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:23.480
Neo gets to decide if he's cool with that,
or if he wants to try to exist on a higher

00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:25.080
plane with his own free will.

00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:29.520
Pretty heady stuff for the multiplex, but
The Matrix features a lot of bells and whistles,

00:04:29.529 --> 00:04:34.569
such as the insane fight between Neo and Agent
Smith, and that innovative "bullet time" effect,

00:04:34.569 --> 00:04:36.160
which seemed to bend time itself.

00:04:36.980 --> 00:04:37.620
Speed

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:42.540
By the mid-'90s, action movies were dying
under their own weight: huge budgets meant

00:04:42.550 --> 00:04:45.669
lots of explosions but not a lot of depth
or character.

00:04:45.669 --> 00:04:51.591
Then came 1994's Speed, an all-killer-no-filler
thrill ride couched in a simple premise: If

00:04:51.591 --> 00:04:57.099
a Los Angeles city bus slows to under 50 miles
per hour, a bomb planted on board explodes.

00:04:57.100 --> 00:05:02.040
It breaks with form to make for lots of surprises,
and the plot necessitates absolutely non-stop

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:02.540
action.

00:05:02.740 --> 00:05:04.160
"We just got a ransom demand from your dead
terrorist.

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:04.940
Says he's rigged a city bus.

00:05:04.940 --> 00:05:05.640
Where's Jack?"

00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:06.800
"Where do you think?"

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:08.320
"I gotta get on that bus."

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:09.320
"You gotta get on … Yeah.

00:05:09.820 --> 00:05:10.320
Yeah!

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:11.620
You get on the bus."

00:05:11.889 --> 00:05:16.200
But there's also a lot of humanity in Speed:
Everyday people from many different walks

00:05:16.210 --> 00:05:20.889
of life are thrust together onto the city
bus, and they come together as a team to rise

00:05:20.889 --> 00:05:22.660
up and meet the challenge.

00:05:22.660 --> 00:05:24.140
Terminator 2: Judgment Day

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:29.569
The original Terminator from 1984 is pretty
fantastic in its own right—dark, gritty,

00:05:29.569 --> 00:05:33.310
weird, and menacing, all with a charming,
low-budget air.

00:05:33.310 --> 00:05:37.999
The 1992 follow-up Terminator 2 was one of
the most expensive movies of all time—but

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:39.920
the money is all up there on the screen.

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:44.820
Especially well executed is the iconic motorcycles
vs. semi-truck chase scene …

00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:56.780
And every time a puddle of liquid metal reshaped
itself into that evil Terminator?

00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:00.720
Still cool, and still looking state of the
art after more than 25 years.

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:02.340
Bullitt

00:06:02.920 --> 00:06:06.900
Steve McQueen was one of the first action
stars, and a pioneer of the form.

00:06:06.909 --> 00:06:11.830
He even did as many of his own stunts as film
studios would let him—for example, he did

00:06:11.830 --> 00:06:17.370
some of the driving for the landmark, on-location
car chase scene in 1968's Bullitt.

00:06:17.370 --> 00:06:22.029
The plot is loaded with twists and intrigue,
and it all culminates as Lt. Frank Bullitt

00:06:22.029 --> 00:06:27.740
chases the bad guys in their 1968 Dodge Charger
through the very real, very hilly streets

00:06:27.740 --> 00:06:28.660
of San Francisco.

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:34.280
No standard issue police cruiser for Bullitt—he's
got a sweet 1968 Ford Mustang GT.

00:06:34.280 --> 00:06:37.000
The high-speed pursuit ends in the best possible
way:

00:06:41.480 --> 00:06:42.720
Mad Max: Fury Road

00:06:43.300 --> 00:06:48.260
Reboots generally don't work—and even if
they do, they're still doomed to pale in comparison

00:06:48.270 --> 00:06:49.380
to the original thing.

00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:54.660
Not so with Mad Max: Fury Road, which expands
and improves on the Mad Max universe with

00:06:54.660 --> 00:07:00.139
a nonstop ride through the familiar, harrowing,
post-apocalyptic wasteland on modified cars

00:07:00.140 --> 00:07:03.560
piloted by crazed, survival-driven nomadic
warriors.

00:07:03.880 --> 00:07:08.620
Mad Max creator George Miller returned to
direct Fury Road, and his 35-plus years of

00:07:08.620 --> 00:07:12.949
experience as a filmmaker are up there on
the screen with an action movie that's both

00:07:12.949 --> 00:07:15.759
endlessly thrilling and emotionally compelling.

00:07:15.759 --> 00:07:20.159
Plus there's a character called "Doof Warrior"
who plays a fire-spewing electric guitar,

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:21.300
if you're still not convinced.

00:07:22.220 --> 00:07:22.880
Gladiator

00:07:23.220 --> 00:07:27.740
"Swords and sandals" movies hadn't been popular
for decades when director Ridley Scott and

00:07:27.740 --> 00:07:31.560
star Russell Crowe brought them back in a
big way with 2000's Gladiator.

00:07:31.980 --> 00:07:36.710
The film won the Academy Award for Best Picture,
and Crowe won Best Actor for his performance

00:07:36.710 --> 00:07:39.400
as Roman general-turned-slave Maximus ...

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:43.960
"Father to a murdered son, husband to a murdered
wife.

00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:46.480
And I will have my vengeance."

00:07:46.760 --> 00:07:51.879
Against a classical Roman backdrop, audiences
root for Maximus's quest to avenge the misdeeds

00:07:51.879 --> 00:07:56.979
of the evil emperor, win his freedom, and
survive the brutal arena — and Scott stages

00:07:56.980 --> 00:07:59.780
some of the most thrilling action sequences
ever put to film.

00:08:00.420 --> 00:08:01.460
Jurassic Park

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:07.659
The original Jurassic Park was a revelation
in 1993, popularizing a subgenre known as

00:08:07.660 --> 00:08:08.640
the "techno-thriller."

00:08:08.960 --> 00:08:14.040
Pioneered by author Michael Crichton, these
fables inevitably involve technology run amok

00:08:14.050 --> 00:08:16.520
to the shock and horror of the humans that
created it.

00:08:16.520 --> 00:08:21.629
"Before you even knew what you had, you patented
it, and packaged it, and slapped it on a plastic

00:08:21.629 --> 00:08:23.960
lunch box, and now (*pounds table*) you're
selling it."

00:08:23.960 --> 00:08:28.279
But of course, that's all a lot of fun to
watch, especially when the technology is realistic

00:08:28.279 --> 00:08:33.180
dinosaurs hunting humans under a majestic,
unforgettable score by John Williams, all

00:08:33.180 --> 00:08:35.900
brought to life with masterful direction by
Steven Spielberg.

00:08:35.900 --> 00:08:39.580
Add it all up, and you've got a modern masterpiece
of popcorn cinema.

00:08:40.460 --> 00:08:41.240
Lethal Weapon

00:08:41.720 --> 00:08:46.350
Detectives Riggs and Murtaugh are mismatched
cops — one a loose cannon who doesn't play

00:08:46.350 --> 00:08:49.580
by the rules, and the other a by-the-books
guy who is ...

00:08:49.580 --> 00:08:51.140
"I'm too old for this s---."

00:08:51.440 --> 00:08:56.240
1987's Lethal Weapon makes this formula work
because the chemistry between Mel Gibson and

00:08:56.240 --> 00:08:57.880
Danny Glover is so charming.

00:08:57.880 --> 00:09:02.310
That, and the plot—largely couched in dark
comedy—never goes where the audience thinks

00:09:02.310 --> 00:09:02.810
it will.

00:09:02.960 --> 00:09:03.700
"You wanna see crazy?

00:09:03.820 --> 00:09:04.360
I'll tell ya."

00:09:08.900 --> 00:09:11.420
"Now that's a real badge, I'm a real cop,
and this is a real f------ gun."

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:14.520
Even thirty years later, this s--- never gets
old.

00:09:15.500 --> 00:09:16.180
The Avengers

00:09:16.500 --> 00:09:20.760
The superhero genre really got going when
Marvel Studios started laying the groundwork

00:09:20.770 --> 00:09:25.780
for its vast cinematic universe, setting the
stage for arguably the single greatest superhero

00:09:25.780 --> 00:09:26.660
team-up possible.

00:09:27.020 --> 00:09:30.980
For the Avengers' long-awaited big-screen
debut, Marvel hired a director who really

00:09:30.990 --> 00:09:35.580
understood comic books—Joss Whedon—and
assembled a cast of acclaimed actors who really

00:09:35.580 --> 00:09:38.010
understood how to deliver Whedon's witty dialogue...

00:09:38.820 --> 00:09:39.820
"Alright, yay.

00:09:40.260 --> 00:09:41.780
Alright, good job, guys.

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:43.480
Let's just not come in tomorrow.

00:09:43.940 --> 00:09:45.400
Let's just take a day."

00:09:45.400 --> 00:09:50.490
No expense was spared making a movie that
was limitless in terms of superpowers, earth-shattering

00:09:50.490 --> 00:09:54.330
fights, things from space—like a comic book
come to life.

00:09:54.330 --> 00:09:59.180
The Avengers is now the standard by which
all other big, fun superhero movies are judged.

00:09:59.900 --> 00:10:00.640
Kill Bill

00:10:01.100 --> 00:10:06.800
Quentin Tarantino's Kill Bill is an epic tale
of revenge, centered on a hero of near-superhuman

00:10:06.800 --> 00:10:11.460
abilities and unrelenting focus, but with
enough vulnerabilities and human motivation

00:10:11.460 --> 00:10:13.190
to make audiences root for her.

00:10:13.190 --> 00:10:18.080
Uma Thurman's Bride goes on a quest to locate
and murder every member of the squad that

00:10:18.080 --> 00:10:22.220
left her for dead years earlier—and find
the baby she was pregnant with at the time

00:10:22.220 --> 00:10:22.820
of the attack.

00:10:23.220 --> 00:10:28.550
The trail ultimately leads to the gang's leader,
and her baby's father, David Carradine's Bill,

00:10:28.550 --> 00:10:33.140
but along the way, the Bride must subdue each
of her enemies in insanely choreographed fight

00:10:33.140 --> 00:10:37.780
sequences, any number of which would be the
centerpiece of any semi-decent action movie.

00:10:37.780 --> 00:10:38.830
Thanks for watching!

00:10:38.830 --> 00:10:41.340
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:10:41.340 --> 00:10:43.070
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

